WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.050
- Good afternoon.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.570
Thank you all for joining
us for this exciting event.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.980
I'm glad to finally
have an audience at YQI

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.370
and you also have some
people joining us online

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.730
and we cannot wait to
welcome actual visitor

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.790
on campus very soon.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:16.900
My name is Florian Carle.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.120
I'm the manager of the
Yale Quantum Institute

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.480
and the curator of the series
of non-technical talks,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.940
co-sponsored by their friends at

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.970
the Franke Program for
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.850
The goal of these events
is to combat science phobia

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.050
and to bring a new
regard to quantum physics

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.710
and science in general,

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.510
by having experts, casts new lights on

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.470
often overlooked aspect
of the scientific work.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:45.290
(indistinct) studying in
the spring semester of 2017

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.020
And we discussed a variety of topics,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.560
among them, the introduction
of music and quantum physics,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.900
art and science, physics and dance,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.710
board games and space exploration,

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.570
mathematic and crochet
and so on and so on.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.340
This evening we have
the pleasure to welcome

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.760
the multimedia video
artist, composer and writer,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.130
Paul Miller also known as DJ Spooky.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:09.370
His work immerse audiences
in a blend of genres,

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.020
global culture,
environmental social issues,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.620
and Paul Miller has collaborated

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.460
with an array of recording artists,

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.070
including Metallica, Chuck
D, Steve Reich and Yoko Ono.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.301
But also with renowned
physicists like Brian Greene,

00:01:22.301 --> 00:01:25.483
Marcelo Gleiser, Stephon
Alexander and Janna Levin.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.330
He's large-scale multimedia
performance pieces

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.370
including "Rebirth of a
Nation," "Terra Nova,"

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.700
that was commissioned by the
Brooklyn Academy of Music,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.310
and the "Seoul
Counterpoints" that he wrote

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.950
during his 2014 residency at
Seoul Institute of the Arts,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.917
and his big multimedia project,

00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:47.144
"Quantopia: The Evolution
of the Internet,"

00:01:47.144 --> 00:01:49.467
that he will talk a little
bit later on during the serc,

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.670
was commissioned by San
Francisco's Archive,

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.210
the Internet Archive in 2019.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.220
We welcome Paul Miller in September,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.010
as a third artist in residence

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.930
at the Yale Quantum Institute.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.510
In this role, Paul Miller will interact

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.410
with the faculty members,
researchers and students,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:06.677
attend our events and colloquia,

00:02:06.677 --> 00:02:10.260
and produce data-driven
multimedia immersive artwork

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.870
which is based on the
work of researcher at YQI

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.610
and their collaboration.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.600
If you're interested
in meeting Paul Miller,

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.830
with him and work with him,

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.002
please reach out to me and
I'll arrange a meeting for you.

00:02:22.002 --> 00:02:22.900
Tonight, Paul will represent

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.660
informal history of invisible things.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.180
The talk will last about
45 minutes to 50 minutes,

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.980
and then we have time for questions.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:30.910
And if you are online,

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.583
please use the Q&amp;A
feature to ask a question.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.245
Please help me welcome
Paul Miller. Thank you.

00:02:37.245 --> 00:02:40.020
- First and foremost, I just want to say,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.000
thanks so much to everyone for joining me.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.260
And I know it's a
gorgeous weather outside,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.470
so it's a real treat
to be with human beings

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:48.830
and you forget that the pandemic,

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.240
it's been a very long 18 months, right?

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.053
I mean, people in a room,
who would have guessed?

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.440
So what I want to do today
is unpack a couple issues

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.270
that will give you guys a
little bit of a foundation

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.890
to some of the work I'll be looking at,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.370
over the next several months,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.670
but also give you a bit of
context to why the relationship

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.550
between Yale Quantum
Institute and me as an artist,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.370
relates to the kind of the
legacy of what I think of

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.820
as interdisciplinary approach
to the sciences and the arts,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.680
and, above all, creating
a more robust conversation

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.500
between science and art

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.420
and of course, the role of the composer

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.123
and artist in the 21st century.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.350
I have a tendency to be very nonlinear,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:32.320
so I'm not going to give you guys

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.723
a normal straight ahead PowerPoint.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.430
That's just the way it is.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.340
All right, we begin with a simple image.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.020
I wanted to begin with this idea

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.000
of pattern recognition in nature.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.920
This is broccoli and amusingly enough,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.920
if you start to think about nature,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.110
biomimicry and mathematics,

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.760
there's always a relationship between

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.840
how we look at pattern recognition

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.380
and how pattern recognition
actually informs

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.460
almost all aspects of human cognition

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.730
and how we put together
the fragments of meaning

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.900
that make up our,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.810
for lack of a word, I'm just
gonna make up a pun here,

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.300
cognitive bandwidth.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.150
And how much we process
the information around us

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.760
is based on, one could argue,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.680
whether you're David Hume
or for that matter Rousseau,

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.360
or fast forward to other philosophers

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.230
like Deleuze Temari or
Jacques Derrida, you name it.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.930
The whole pun here is
that pattern recognition

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.910
is how human beings navigate

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.260
the very complex world around us

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.240
and how it gives us grounding.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.700
In a world, that one could argue,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.550
is filled with uncertainties,

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.270
even about the basic humanity

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.320
and the way that we relate to

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.380
this complex universe around us.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.700
So if we jump from broccoli to fractals,

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.620
this is a Mandelbrot set,

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.000
you can easily see that there's a kind of

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.350
a compelling relationship
between mathematics and nature

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.130
and, above all, nature
as the human evolves

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.570
over the last several
hundred thousand years,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.400
we can easily see that we've been

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.530
in this process of trying to,

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.210
there's a very wonderful Israeli
philosopher, Yuval Harari.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.800
If you haven't read
"Sapiens," it's a great book.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.270
Human beings have been
involved with this notion of

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.590
sort of suppressing aspects of nature

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.960
as we move further into the 21st century

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.340
and the industrial revolution gave way to,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.270
one could argue, an
information revolution.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.600
And now as we move further
into a data-driven society,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.700
we'll be seeing far more engagements

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.700
with disruption of nature

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.740
and of course that's
where environmentalist

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.930
call now the Anthropocene era.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.990
So math, nature and, above
all, pattern recognition.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.840
These are things that are compelling,

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.810
but also deeply disturbed.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.200
The psychologist Sigmund Freud

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.053
would call this the
uncanny, the unheimlich,

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:45.870
it's a German term for meaning unholy

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.733
or at the edge of the familiar.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.800
For many in the audience, this
is a simple kind of graph,

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.370
diagram of a mathematical process.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.060
But if you bounce it off of nature

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.410
and you see the same thing

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.700
and how this vegetable, for example,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.150
was just optimizing its
relationship to sunlight,

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.820
to be able to optimize its
relationship to resources

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.850
and, above all, create, one
could argue, a tasty meal.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.520
It means that nature and humans,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.290
we are not separate and, above all,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.490
the 19th century, one could argue,

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.060
took us down a strange
path of industrialization

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.920
and the idea of scarcity

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.520
and, above all, this notion
that capitalism had set up

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.770
this notion of a deterministic universe.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:30.210
Which leads me to this gentleman.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.213
This is Simon Pierre Laplace.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.800
He is a gentlemen who felt
that at the beginning of,

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.680
maybe the middle of the 18th century.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.020
I say the beginning, maybe
the late 18th century.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.763
There was, above all, a
relationship to revolution.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.050
One could argue the
French and the Americans

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.780
and many of the other global cultures

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.740
were involved with a series of upheavals

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.330
that many environmentalists have traced

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:59.170
to what they call the short ice age.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.407
There was a tremendous
amount of crop failure

00:07:01.407 --> 00:07:02.790
in the mid-18th century,

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:06.050
because a volcanic eruption had sent

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:09.630
a tremendous amount of
particles into the atmosphere.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.300
And so winters were colder and longer

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.400
and summers were kind of faint.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.690
And so there's a lot of people who feel

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:18.370
that the 18th century upheavals,

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.840
including the revolutions,

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.230
can be tied directly to climate change.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.420
But he wrote an essay that's gonna inform

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:25.930
some of the discussions today.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:27.987
The English translation
is just simply called

00:07:27.987 --> 00:07:31.260
"A Philosophical Essay on Probabilities."

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.600
And he felt that you could actually,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.810
if you had enough computational power,

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.600
that you would be able
to predict the movement

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.500
of every particle making
up the entire universe.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.020
Now here in the 21st century

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.270
our notion of computational realism,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.730
of being able to actually
have the computational power

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.290
to be able to engage
this kind of thinking,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.930
it's a pretty powerful moment.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.000
And one can argue that the 18th
century deterministic model,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.880
whether it's Newtonian,
classical, physics,

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.650
or whether, later on,

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:04.460
one could argue that this
kind of deterministic thinking

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.020
set the tone for the 20th century's idea

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.740
of the industrial psychology

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:12.370
that led to all other
elements being disrupted.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.840
When I say all the other
elements, for example,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.150
humans and nature,

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.510
the idea of the human
place as we move further

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:19.930
into the Anthropocene

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.340
and, above all, the idea that patterns

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.500
were rigidly controlled and deterministic.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.260
The idea of freewill, for
example, and Facebook,

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.450
freewill and Facebook.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:32.410
And obviously there's a
huge scandal right now,

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.230
but if you look at Cambridge Analytica

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:38.400
and many of the other data mining

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.340
and data surveillance industries,

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.930
they're based on this idea
that it's relatively trivial

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.940
to compute how human beings engage

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.360
with the information around them

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.210
and the patterns around them.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:52.410
And thus things like
Instagram, Facebook, TikTok,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.160
these are all computational machineries

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.360
that are engaged with manipulation

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.940
and, above all, sort of
colonizing the subconscious

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:03.230
as it goes further into
a data-driven narrative.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.760
So Pierre-Simon Laplace,

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:06.770
I'll just read this out loud,

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.100
just so everyone can be
on the same page here,

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.560
it says, "We may regard the present state

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.960
of the universe as the effect of its past

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.470
and the cause of its future.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:16.330
An intellect, which at a certain moment,

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:19.070
would know all forces
that set nature in motion

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:22.600
and all positions of all items
of which nature is composed,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.430
if this intellect were also vast enough

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:27.800
to submit these data to analysis,

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.590
it would embrace in a single formula

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:32.050
the movements of the greatest
bodies of the universe.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:33.870
and those are the tiniest atom,

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.530
for such an intellect
nothing would be uncertain

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.470
and the future just like the past

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.510
would be present before its eyes."

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.780
Now, whether you're an Aristotelian

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:46.490
or someone who believes
in the platonic ideals of,

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.700
the foundations of Western philosophy,

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.930
this still relates to how
we think about structure

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.650
and its relationship to how a determinist

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:59.160
versus indeterminist, a sort
of cognitive process works.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.660
Other philosophers like
Rousseau, for example,

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:03.950
we call this, the unmoved mover.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.970
A Daemon, a demon, D-A-E-M-O-N,

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:09.790
that would imaginarily create a structure

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.530
where you'd have to always be guessing,

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.210
but guess what?

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.190
You could fast forward to Schrodinger

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:16.680
and other kinds of physicists

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.440
who are still influenced by this

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.500
and Schrodinger's infamous cat,

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.630
where you're able to think about

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:26.060
the idea of randomness
and chance operations,

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.240
all of this still lingers
over the conversation.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.110
From these 18th century philosophers,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.610
Rousseau, Laplace and so on,

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.910
you can easily see that
there was a deep tension

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:38.600
in philosophy and science

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.610
and of course, Newton navigated that

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.710
and led the path for people like Einstein.

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.780
Let's give you guys a
little bit of context.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.430
I'm an artist, a writer, a musician

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.490
who's deeply enmeshed in thinking

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.090
about the conversation
between science and the arts.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.670
My father was Dean of Howard
University's Law School.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:00.448
I grew up in Washington,
DC in the ancient 1970s.

00:11:00.448 --> 00:11:02.350
So I'll just show you guys quick,

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:06.570
this is Dupont Circle in
Washington, DC in the early '70s.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.300
My mother was a historian of design

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.223
and my father was Dean of
Howard University's Law School.

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:13.480
There was a tremendous amount

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.120
of political activity at that time,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.220
you had J. Edgar Hoover, you had Nixon,

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.570
Vietnam War is raging.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.480
Some of the cases my father
represented, for example,

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.850
he was a lawyer for Angela Davis

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.490
and the Black Panthers, very political.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:29.170
So our household was near Dupont Circle.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.490
I don't know if anybody here knows DC,

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.990
but growing up in that region, there was,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:35.450
you had the embassies,

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.770
you had a very international
cosmopolitan scene.

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:42.720
I had friends from China,
Russia, India, Japan.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.680
So growing up in a downtown
Washington DC context,

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.460
we also had, there was a
vegetarian punk scene, for example.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:51.540
I don't know if you guys
know bands like Fugazi,

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.300
which was, or Minor Threat, or Bad Brains.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.980
Anyone who's into thrash punk.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:59.830
I don't know, you guys
might not be into them,

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.640
but it was popular at the time.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.810
So growing up in a cosmopolitan situation

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.630
made me realize that
the idea that science,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.120
the arts and history,

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:14.320
were all kind of in this
American uneasiness.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:16.370
And as we've seen during the pandemic,

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.500
we're not just facing
a biological pandemic,

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.810
we're facing an ideological
and political crisis

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:23.500
based on the crisis of democracy

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.090
as it collides with the social media

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.370
and Facebook and Twitter
and Instagram itself.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.460
So going back to that time period,

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:34.740
the legacies I'm inherited
are from that kind of era.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.710
Critique of the political discourse,

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.950
thinking about human rights,
thinking about history,

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:43.730
and this disruptive
innovation that was going on

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:46.470
as the world became more and more digital.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:50.210
So fast forward to our current moment,

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:51.780
this is some of my artwork.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.950
This is a fun remix of Lewis Carroll.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:58.320
I tend to always
incorporate found materials.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.110
This is some of my visual artwork.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:01.960
I'm a big fan of Lewis Carroll

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.040
and obviously he was
someone who would play

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.020
with the mythology of mathematics

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.890
and many of his works,

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.580
of course "Alice in
Wonderland" are foundational

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.730
to, one could argue, this sort
of computational narratives

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.063
that we inhabit now.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.520
In fact, the science fiction
writer, Neal Stephenson,

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.250
if you haven't read his work,
he's a wonderful writer,

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:22.670
his book, "Snow Crash," for example.

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.510
He coined the term, the metaverse,

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.971
which we haven't encountered.

00:13:26.971 --> 00:13:29.360
It's well worth exploring.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.980
So when you start to think
about the visual arts,

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:35.760
many of my other friends
in this kind of context

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.293
are artists such as Shep Fairey.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:44.050
Shep, for example, made
the Obama "Hope" poster,

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:48.440
which became very popular
when Obama was running.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:52.340
There's also, all sorts of
other artists who I think...

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:53.430
Shep does "Obey."

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:57.700
He went to RISD and I went to
Bowdoin College up in Maine.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:59.810
So just giving you guys
a little bit of context,

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:00.770
if you haven't seen his work,

00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:02.037
you probably seen the stickers that say,

00:14:02.037 --> 00:14:03.737
"Andre the giant has a posse."

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:07.890
We've known each other
since we were in college,

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.940
I went to Bowdoin and
RISD had better parties,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:14.150
so we would drive down
there on the weekends.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:16.460
All right, you have a
little bit of context.

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.730
And now, how does this relate to science?

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:22.620
Let's give you guys a little
bit of a interpretation.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:24.770
There's a very dear friend of
mine named Margaret Atwood.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:26.660
If you haven't read her work,

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:29.480
currently, the world is
feeling like one of her books,

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:32.030
actually, "The Handmaid's Tale."

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.190
Margaret is a science fiction writer

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.290
who uses feminist narratives to critique

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.120
how we think about the near future

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.057
and control of women's bodies.

00:14:40.057 --> 00:14:43.760
"The Handmaiden's Tale" is a
absolute classic hands-down,

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:44.650
if you haven't read it,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.640
but she inspires a lot
of my thinking right now

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.780
about the legacy of the body

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.981
as it orientates in this world of,

00:14:51.981 --> 00:14:55.143
how should I put this,
of coercive technologies.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:00.050
Some of my other work is presented

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:03.190
in the context of
installations at museums,

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:05.270
galleries and things like that.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:06.810
One of my favorite concerts,

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.860
and again I'm just using
this as an example,

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.730
was we got the Greek government to give me

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:12.340
the Acropolis for an evening

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:16.553
and I projected, throughout
the ruins of the Acropolis,

00:15:16.553 --> 00:15:19.820
a very infamous film by D.W. Griffith,

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:21.300
called "Birth of a Nation."

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.160
And for those who are into film history,

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.020
the film is deeply controversial,

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.373
but it's considered the DNA of Hollywood.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.960
Basically, it's a film that shows,

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:33.773
it's a KKK propaganda film.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:37.670
Just for the record, just to clarify,

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:39.030
just to tell you guys as an audience,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:40.873
I can assure you I'm not a KKK member.

00:15:40.873 --> 00:15:42.410
(woman laughing)

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:44.200
But basically when I was in negotiations

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.877
with the Greek government, they were like,

00:15:45.877 --> 00:15:49.450
"But why do you want to play
a KKK film at the Acropolis?"

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:52.050
and I was like, "That's a good question."

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.380
So I was like, "Look, you guys are Greek.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:54.890
You have comedy, tragedy."

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.540
and they're like, "Ah, I get it, okay."

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:59.100
So what they did was
they allowed me to put

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.750
a sound system throughout the ruins

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:03.710
and what we ended up doing was projecting

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.190
different versions of the
film throughout the ruins.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:08.260
So the audience would come into,

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.130
this is called the
Herodes Atticus theater,

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:11.730
it's the beginning of modern theater,

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:12.820
if you think about it.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:13.960
This kind of semi-circle,

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:15.280
you can still see that as a logo

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.350
for our modern movie
theaters, for example.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:19.337
You might be thinking,

00:16:19.337 --> 00:16:20.760
"What does this have to do with science?"

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:21.990
I'm building up to that.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.060
But when you start to think about,

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:25.930
these kind of temple of reason,

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:28.170
Athena and Heracles,

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.220
and the idea that at that time

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.760
democracy was very fragile

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:36.100
and the competition between
Sparta and Athens had generated

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.740
this intense, for lack of a better word,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.463
critique of the individual and the state.

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.150
So later on the Romans came in

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.120
and obviously they took over,

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:48.970
but this theater became the basic premise

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.690
of how rhetoric and how exchange of ideas,

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.560
if you could look on further, of course,

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.460
people like Aristotle, Plato,

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:57.030
they would be hanging out in this area

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.963
and there was an area called
the Agora, the public space.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.867
So somebody would jump
up on a stone and say,

00:17:02.867 --> 00:17:04.000
"This is my philosophy."

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.227
And then somebody else down
the road would be saying,

00:17:06.227 --> 00:17:08.680
"Hey, this is my philosophy," and so on.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.290
So what lingers over the
conversation today is Pythagoras.

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:13.820
I'm a huge fan of him.

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:16.440
He was also a controversial figure.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.530
And for those who are into
the history of mathematics,

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:19.660
he's generally considered to be

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:23.190
one of the first ancient
philosophers to be able

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:26.373
to combine tuning systems and math.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:30.800
The thing about Greeks culture's
that they didn't have zero

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:33.420
and zero obviously is incredibly important

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:36.040
in the evolution of how
we think about the arts,

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:37.790
how we think about tuning systems

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:40.850
and how we think about
our modern binary code

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:43.050
that drives every digital
device around you,

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:43.883
zeroes and ones.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:49.580
Pythagoras was a deeply troubled
person, one could argue.

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:52.450
He had a cult of
mathematicians around him.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:53.900
You were not allowed to see his face.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.820
He would speak through a veil

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:58.053
and he would only speak
to you in equations.

00:17:59.131 --> 00:18:01.070
If you were not able to
answer the equations,

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:02.620
you were not worth speaking to.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.930
His most feared thing was the
square root of negative one.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:09.500
As a matter of fact, there's rumors that

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:10.740
he would have you assassinated

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.470
if you spoke a square root
of negative one aloud,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:16.020
because he felt that it
would unravel the universe.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:20.880
But what inspired Pythagoras
as an ancient Greek philosopher

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:23.750
was this tension between
the public space and math.

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:26.100
And if you look at, I'll
give you another example,

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.270
the Oracle of Delphi for example,

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.830
all of this goes to the
heart of Western mathematics

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:34.120
as tied into the social
process, nothing was separate.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:36.700
As one could argue, that
interdisciplinary foundation

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:39.170
of Western math, inspired architecture,

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:42.120
inspired philosophy, inspired
pretty much everything

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:44.093
that currently drives our society.

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.970
I'm giving you a little
bit of context on this

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:50.630
as a way of showing how art
can intervene in architecture.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:53.060
Art can also intervene
in the social process

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:56.460
and, above all, how we think
about contemporary practice

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.483
as an interdisciplinary space.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.050
There's plenty of other art projects,

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:04.720
but I want to give you guys

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:08.260
a little bit of context as
well, just for that arc.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:11.730
This is a very famous situation from 1915,

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.350
it's the Italian Futurists.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.300
The gentleman on the
left with the mustache,

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.460
his name's Luigi Russolo

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.140
and basically at this time,

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:24.270
he came up with this series
of projects called "Futurism."

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:27.270
And basically, the Futurist movement

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:32.270
wanted to do invisible portraits
of a accelerated landscape

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.010
and he felt that the
science of the imagination,

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:38.520
literally we call it the
science of the imagination,

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:40.400
was gonna be about the tension of how

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:43.730
a culture was accelerating
because of industrialization

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:46.780
and he would have these Futurist concerts.

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:49.240
In 1915 the audience
would walk into your room,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:52.340
much like this and the
concert would be coming

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.360
out of these crazy speakers.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:57.450
And so the audience would riot,

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.910
people would throw bottles at him,

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:02.800
they were booing and they
would say, "Where's the band?"

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.280
People were very upset that the music

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.030
was coming out of these speakers

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:09.660
and he was like, "The music of the future

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:10.880
will all be coming out of this."

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:11.960
And if you can look above you

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.683
and see all those speakers,
you can see who won.

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:19.210
From 1915 to our time period,

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:20.550
you can usually see that the arts

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:22.820
have had a nonlinear relationship,

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:24.640
both with the industrial process,

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.130
with how people think
of the creative economy

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:29.440
and, above all, how science informs

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:31.110
the practice of this notion

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.223
of collapsing the silos between mediums.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.390
Another project that I just
want to bring to your attention,

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.100
I went to Antarctica for six
weeks, a little while ago,

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:42.620
and there was a group of scientists

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:45.086
working on a project called "Ice Cube."

00:20:45.086 --> 00:20:48.760
Antarctica has some of the
best landscapes in the world

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:51.330
for studying the interaction of neutrinos.

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.930
And what's beautiful about this project,

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.970
was I got a chance to really go deep

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.080
into these larger landscapes of ice.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:00.090
And so when I was there,

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:02.640
I was doing what I call
acoustic portraits of ice

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.330
and ice is a stunningly
beautiful material.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:07.540
It's quite, I'll show you in a moment,

00:21:07.540 --> 00:21:09.380
it's quite mathematical.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:13.020
But you can easily see this
is a landscape of devastation.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:15.350
The landscape is collapsing

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:17.270
and you can easily see in the collapse,

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:20.200
it's scraping the entire side
of a mountain, for example.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.010
Now, most of North America was carved

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:23.140
by the retreat of the glaciers

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.120
in the last several thousand years

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:26.300
and one could argue that

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:29.260
we're in an accelerated,
again, Anthropocene era

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:31.750
where nature and disruption of, obviously,

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:34.700
this summer was the wildest
summer in human history,

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:37.890
forest fires, crazy weather patterns.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:40.220
So buckle up, it's gonna get a lot deeper.

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:43.870
And going to Antarctica
from the heart of New York

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:46.460
and then doing, what I call,
these acoustic portraits

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:50.330
was a real eye-opener about
the legacy of deep time.

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:53.040
What you're seeing here is
tens of millions of years

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.880
vanishing in the space
of a couple of decades.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:57.560
And so we're looking at a collision

00:21:57.560 --> 00:21:59.930
of human time and deep time.

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:02.580
And so as a composer,
how do you engage that?

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.260
How do you think about this
notion of pattern recognition

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.100
lingering over an entire
rise of a species,

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:10.970
our species, in such a
short amount of time.

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:12.830
So we're seeing the acceleration

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.040
and disruption of the entire biome

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:17.100
and, above all, we're also seeing

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.120
that the human sense of agency

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:23.750
has now created a way of
thinking about our imagination

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:26.970
as applied to the entire
universe around us.

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:29.340
And one could argue, Laplace, Rousseau,

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:30.830
maybe they weren't so far off.

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:32.300
Maybe we are imagining it all

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:35.503
and our intervention in
this is quite intriguing.

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:40.750
Most of my books are
published with MIT Press

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.060
and I've been working
with MIT for many years.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:45.850
The current book that I'm working on now

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:48.080
is called, "Digital Fictions."

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:50.250
I just wanna show you this really quickly.

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:52.600
This is a map, it's a
little blurry for you,

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:53.610
I'll just zoom in really quickly.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:55.850
It's called the acoustic wave spectrum

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:57.460
and I used this in my last book

00:22:57.460 --> 00:22:58.670
because I wanted to show people

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.770
a macro versus micro level wave forms.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.350
Imagine a wave form that can,

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.740
I'll just zoom in, so
hopefully you can see this,

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:08.680
a wave form that would
last over a million years.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.510
So you're looking at oceanic cycles,

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:12.690
you're looking at tectonic plate movement.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:14.700
You're looking at, I'll
just zoom out here,

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:16.360
biospheric cycles.

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.440
And then maybe we'll go down in the scale,

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:20.350
for millions of years,

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.130
to maybe a pattern that might
last over several months,

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:27.830
global wave, seasonal cycles and so on.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:30.000
Planetary cycles rotation of, you know,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.180
different phenomenon and atmosphere.

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:35.550
Then we zoom down to the frequency

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:38.400
in cycles to the human range,

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.520
which is usually, our
hearing is between one

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.670
and 100,000 cycles per second.

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:47.120
We hear and can interact with
these kinds of wave forms,

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:50.120
mechanical oscillation, surface waves,

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:53.130
what they call capitation, conical waves,

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:55.210
sound waves that obviously,

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.600
a truck going by or whatever.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.220
And then if we go below the human range

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.410
down to a micro acoustic level, you know,

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:04.590
millions of cycles per second,

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:07.210
hundreds of thousands
of cycles per second,

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:09.240
you can easily start to
see that it starts getting

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:14.130
into the realm of quantum
mechanics and other forms of,

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.230
thinking about these micro,
micro, micro, micro levels

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:20.330
and the wave forms then become far more,

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:22.023
what you call electroacoustic,

00:24:22.023 --> 00:24:25.240
magnetoacoustic, photonic and so on.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:28.120
And the human ear, obviously,

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.580
is not evolved to engage this mechanism,

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:34.060
but technology, given our current process,

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.240
we've been able to engage
these kinds of wave forms

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:39.300
and being able to then use them

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:41.420
for all sorts of different approaches.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.030
Every device you have in your pocket,

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:45.780
your cell phone, lights,

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:48.390
all of this comes out of
the engagement between,

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:52.880
obviously, the last century's
notions of quantum mechanics

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:54.800
and then updated through the filter

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:57.670
of what became a consumer
culture based on that.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:01.970
These wave forms are all
part of everyday life,

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:04.840
but at the same time, there's
a deep physics approach

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:08.023
to how the human ear helps us
navigate the world around us.

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:12.160
Each of those is different
kinds of processes

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:14.630
and I want to make sure
that as an audience,

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:16.710
you realize that this is information

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:18.630
that can lead you down many paths

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:19.683
and I celebrate that.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:21.940
There's one of my favorite composers,

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:25.260
also a Greek composer,
his name is Iannis Xenakis

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:27.530
and he began a kind of approach

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:29.350
where he incorporated algorithms

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:30.959
and he called this style of composition,

00:25:30.959 --> 00:25:33.444
the stochastic method.

00:25:33.444 --> 00:25:35.390
Here we are at Yale Quantum Institute

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:37.230
and stochastic processes,

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:40.000
obviously entropy and
other kinds of things.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:42.890
Those are, again, part of this
notion of quantum engagement

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:44.550
and how do we think about that?

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:47.360
But he would take ancient
Greek mathematic formulas

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:48.900
and turn them into symphonies.

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:51.920
Me and him did a series of collaborations.

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:53.590
And this is the beginning of my career,

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:56.500
I had, obviously, a lot
more hair back then.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.400
But the fun part about
the way the ancient Greeks

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.950
would think about math in the everyday

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:03.590
was that it was part of
this emotional engagement

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:05.920
with how you thought about intervening

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:07.253
and pattern recognition.

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:10.650
He's one of my favorite composers

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:12.630
and his work influences my thinking a lot.

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:14.490
Again, he's one of the first composers

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.103
to use algorithms to generate symphonies.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.600
Let's go to a little
bit more current stuff.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:22.980
One project I'm working on now,

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.947
is what you call
computational neuroscience.

00:26:24.947 --> 00:26:27.997
And what I've been doing is
working on brainwave patterns.

00:26:27.997 --> 00:26:30.050
The human mind generates generally

00:26:30.050 --> 00:26:31.450
five wave form frequencies

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.660
that are easy to quantify and measure.

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:36.080
Delta, theta, alpha, beta, gamma.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:39.880
So obviously each of those
is a frequency wave form

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:41.710
that the human ear doesn't pick up,

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:43.310
but your brain is generating it.

00:26:44.149 --> 00:26:46.450
And what I've been doing over the summer

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:49.930
is working on mapping
out brain wave patterns

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:52.290
and then using those to generate sound

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.890
and then being able to balance meditation

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.170
or other approaches to thinking

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:58.720
about the brain and wave forms.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:00.040
This is a rough draft

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.310
and this is just an acoustic portrait

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:05.050
of me thinking about some hip-hop.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:07.640
So at the moment, this is a rough draft,

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.790
it'll get more intense, probably 2022.

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:12.720
But I'm giving you guys little projects

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:14.840
that can hopefully give
you a bit of context

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:17.810
about why I'm here with
the Yale Quantum Institute.

00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:20.610
All right, so that's one project.

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:22.570
And then I did want to make sure

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:24.760
that you guys can also think about,

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.493
a little bit of the history
of what inspires my work.

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:30.030
I collect vinyl,

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:31.590
this is a very famous collaboration

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.500
between two of my favorite composers,

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:35.590
Sun Ra and John Cage.

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:38.630
John Cage most famously created a piece

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.690
called, "Four minutes, 30 seconds."

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:42.607
Or is it "Four minutes, 33 seconds?"

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:45.970
It's a famous piece
about silence, basically.

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:49.160
And Sun Ra is a legendary
figure who opened up

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:52.060
the idea of noise and free jazz.

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:54.240
The reason I think these two and physics,

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:57.580
is both of them were inspired
by interstellar phenomena

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:59.750
and the idea of silence.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:01.060
This is very rare record.

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.230
I've been collecting
vinyls since I was a kid

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:05.180
and for me, at least half of this stuff

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:08.730
is always about the notion of the archive,

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:10.030
how you engage your archive,

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:11.910
how you reactivate your archive

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:14.593
and then how that becomes
a collage process.

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:18.310
These are all just bits and pieces.

00:28:18.310 --> 00:28:20.930
And by the way, I could easily
put this in a slide format,

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:22.750
but I'm sick of PowerPoint,

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.903
so I like the idea of kind
of a non-linear scroll here.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:28.740
Over the last couple of months,

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:31.300
I've been finishing a series of books

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:33.030
and some of my favorite authors,

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:35.430
this is a gentleman by the
name of William Gibson.

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:37.100
If you're not familiar with his work,

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:39.370
he coined the term
cyberspace, for example,

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:42.040
and his most famous book
is called "Neuromancer."

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:46.410
I'm very intrigued by the
science fictional relationship,

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:49.870
right now moving further
into a data-driven society

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:51.390
and there's, say for example,

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:53.120
there's a Chinese author
who's a big influence

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:55.340
on my work right now, Cixin Liu.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:57.140
He wrote this book,
"The Three-Body Problem"

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:58.913
or "The Dark Forest" trilogy.

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:03.020
Gibson in the idea of cyberspace.

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.720
Again, Neal Stephenson is
another science fiction writer

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:09.510
who influences my thinking
with his term, the metaverse.

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:12.660
All of which means that the
science fictional relationship

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:14.000
to the world around us.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:17.450
Other authors like Isaac
Asimov's or Philip K. Dick

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.933
also inform and inspire some of my work.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:24.360
Let's see, I wanna make sure
that you guys are getting

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.620
a little bit of a broad spectrum.

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:29.400
I've been also doing some volunteer work

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:31.360
with reef restorations,

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:34.320
because I'm fascinated
with how ocean currents

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.380
and heat and temperature differentials

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:39.110
can actually generate this idea,

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:41.490
again, of living through
pattern recognition,

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.290
but also thinking about,

00:29:43.290 --> 00:29:45.680
if the Anthropocene and radical disruption

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.203
of how nature is being wiped out by human,

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:51.933
just human location or pacing.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:56.580
I do scuba diving and this is
a series of fun initiatives

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:01.580
where we're dealing with
putting reefs back together.

00:30:02.915 --> 00:30:05.060
There's an island called Bonaire
that I go to occasionally

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:09.760
and I also go to Vanuatu and
Solomon Islands, Tahiti, Fiji,

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:13.780
and what we do is look at
different reef destruction

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:17.640
and then try and reboot
them with either grafts,

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.680
where you can have different reefs

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:21.200
from different regions of the world

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:22.373
and help them heal.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:24.930
It's fun, it's interesting,

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:25.840
but you can easily see

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:28.720
there's a mathematical
relationship going on here

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:33.350
and the way that the coral
reefs are trying to optimize

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:36.340
their relationship to the ocean
currents and the salinity,

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.870
the temperature
differentials, the sunlight.

00:30:38.870 --> 00:30:41.407
Regretfully, the planet
is on fire right now

00:30:41.407 --> 00:30:43.123
and in the climate crisis is,

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:47.890
that's a whole different topic.

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:49.510
But I'm heavily involved with a lot

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:52.213
of climate active justice issues.

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:56.100
This is one of my favorite
projects at the moment

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:58.780
and actually we're gonna be,
hopefully, going back there,

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:01.010
or I will be going back later this year

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:03.200
to do a series of sculptural projects

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:05.900
and looking at how to sculpt the coral

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:08.560
so that it can both be
visually compelling,

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:12.560
but also help it grow
in a more robust way.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:15.330
And again, these are radically

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:16.670
different projects I'm showing you guys

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:18.390
and I'm giving you guys
the cliff-notes version

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:19.390
just 'cause of time.

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:22.733
Okay. Let's see really quick.

00:31:24.271 --> 00:31:25.520
I'm trying as much as possible

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:26.840
to give you guys a sensibility,

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:28.600
both how I do research,

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:31.930
but also how that research
then informs my process.

00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:34.279
This is a gentleman who is
also a big inspiration to me,

00:31:34.279 --> 00:31:37.020
his name is Al-Khawarizmi and
if you don't know his work,

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:39.520
he was in the eighth and ninth century

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:41.980
in Baghdad at the House of Wisdom

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.290
and he coined the term, algorithm,

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:48.500
which most Westerners do not
know, it's an Arabic term.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.880
And the Arabs had borrowed these numbers,

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:54.260
including zero from the Hindu

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:57.740
and their notion of Sanskrit mathematics.

00:31:57.740 --> 00:32:01.410
So his term basically,

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:03.410
became the foundation of Western math

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.610
because of Fibonacci.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:09.490
And Musa Al-Khawarizmi,
generally is considered to be,

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:11.790
the other derivative of
his name is Al-Devore.

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:17.770
So one could argue that the
conversation between east, west,

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.520
between Islam and science

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:23.850
and the way Muslim culture
actually helped preserve

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:27.930
a lot of records that the
ancient Greeks and Romans had,

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:30.140
they had taken one path in mathematics,

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:32.920
but had not had zero, for example.

00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:35.860
Which leads me to a conversation

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:38.550
that inspires pretty much
this entire residency

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:41.153
and I wanna make sure foreground this.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:49.440
2019 was the 50th
anniversary of the internet

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:51.720
and I was commissioned to do a symphony

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.730
celebrating this 50th anniversary.

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:58.680
So from 1969 to 2019, the
internet basically reached out

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:02.090
and changed the way we think
about pretty much everything.

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:05.550
This is the internet in
1969, there was four hubs.

00:33:05.550 --> 00:33:09.700
The first two hubs went
live in October, 1969.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.540
And again, you guys are based in physics

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:16.330
so we can easily see a
exponential notion of growth.

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:18.730
From October to December,

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:20.650
the first two hubs where between UCLA

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:23.590
and Stanford Research Institute,
you can see those two,

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:25.420
and then by December you have four,

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:28.220
then keep going, keep going, keep going.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:29.590
Next thing you know, you have billions

00:33:29.590 --> 00:33:31.330
and billions and billions of nodes.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:33.990
But if you were a composer looking at

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:35.780
that notion of exponential growth,

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:37.930
how would you turn that
into a composition?

00:33:39.612 --> 00:33:41.880
I was commissioned by
the Internet Archive,

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:44.820
which is a very incredible organization

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:47.300
out of San Francisco, UCLA,

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.790
and the San Francisco Young Women's Choir.

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:54.510
I reached out to the gentleman
who wrote this equation,

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:56.920
and I just want to make
sure you're aware of this,

00:33:56.920 --> 00:33:58.610
generally this is the equation that set up

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:00.000
what you call packet switching,

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.070
which allows the entire internet to work.

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:04.360
And the idea was the US
Military, through ARPANET,

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:06.780
needed a network to be
able to send messages

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.640
in case there was a nuclear war

00:34:08.640 --> 00:34:10.120
or if there was other disruption

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:12.050
of major communication systems.

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:15.370
So he figured out a clever
way to break a message

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:19.100
into component elements
based on zeroes and ones.

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:21.300
And essentially what I ended
up doing was working with him.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:23.340
Dr. Leonard Kleinrock, if you look him up,

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:24.340
he's generally considered to be

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:26.140
one of the founders of the internet.

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:30.880
End up working with him to do a research

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:34.710
into how we think about
message component systems

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:37.710
and I then took that and made it become,

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:39.913
so this is me and Dr. Leonard Kleinrock,

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:45.710
and I took his equations
and then had a chorus,

00:34:45.710 --> 00:34:49.010
the San Francisco Young
Women's Choir sing it.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:52.000
And I'll just show you
what that would look like.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:55.010
Each young woman is singing
parts of the equation

00:34:55.010 --> 00:34:57.310
and then I had a string
quartet that I'm sampling,

00:34:57.310 --> 00:34:58.560
running through my software.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.090
And this was presented at UCLA.

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:03.180
And eventually what happens is you have

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:05.130
more and more and more nodes.

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:06.910
And the women are all wearing black

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:09.460
and we're projecting on them

00:35:09.460 --> 00:35:11.930
and I'd taken the equation
and then run it through

00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:14.670
a alogarithmic series of growth patterns.

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.250
So you can usually see its relationship

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:18.770
to the exponential growth of the internet.

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:21.350
So you're hearing all
these young women's voices,

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:23.340
sort of cascading around the room

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:26.250
and then sampling the string quartet

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:28.350
through various software that I use.

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:30.390
So this is just one project.

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:31.910
But this is called "Quantopia,"

00:35:31.910 --> 00:35:34.193
which simply stands for Quantified Utopia.

00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:36.840
Each of the projects
I'm presenting to you,

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:39.020
you're getting a three
to five minute version,

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.000
but generally that take
months of research,

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:42.217
you have to work with
people who commissioned it

00:35:42.217 --> 00:35:43.730
and you have to work with,

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:45.360
it's not every day you can be a composer

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:47.040
and walk in and talk
to the Greek government

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:49.103
and get the Acropolis, I'm just saying.

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:51.510
So each of these takes

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:53.700
a tremendous amount of research and work

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:56.600
and it then becomes a
concert, or an installation,

00:35:56.600 --> 00:35:58.010
or a series of conversations

00:35:58.010 --> 00:36:00.870
with data scientists or other artists.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.570
The reason I want to show you guys,

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:04.860
these are all totally different projects,

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:08.360
is that there has to be a
conversation across silos

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:11.010
and what the problem with
early 21st century is,

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.370
many of us are in our own silo.

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:14.203
As a matter of fact,

00:36:14.203 --> 00:36:16.640
if you're a Facebook
group or this or that,

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:19.270
mathematically speaking,
you're just in what,

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:21.030
there's a theoretician named Eli Pariser,

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.550
who call the filter bubble.

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:24.410
You just keep getting the same thing,

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:26.970
because you're in your silo.

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:29.490
I want to break down those silos
and I'd like to communicate

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:32.460
across mediums and across disciplines.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:34.720
So that's what this residency is about

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:37.130
and I'll be working with

00:36:37.130 --> 00:36:39.510
the Yale Quantum Institute
over the next several months

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:41.450
and working with scientists

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:43.290
and other people from your community

00:36:43.290 --> 00:36:48.030
to figure out a compelling way
to compose and generate art

00:36:48.030 --> 00:36:51.410
based on this idea of
breaking down those silos.

00:36:51.410 --> 00:36:53.460
One of the inspirations for the residency

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.320
is a conversation between
these two gentlemen.

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:58.750
On the right you have Rabindranath Tagore,

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:01.900
who is a Bengali Hindu poet

00:37:02.878 --> 00:37:04.520
and on the left was Einstein.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:08.590
These guys met backstage
at the Nobel Prize Awards

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:10.070
and stayed in touch.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.900
They had the coolest haircuts in the room.

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:13.733
(woman laughing)

00:37:13.733 --> 00:37:15.460
And what ended up
happening is they just got

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:17.490
into a series of debates and discussions

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.957
and Einstein said to
Rabindranath Tagore famously,

00:37:20.957 --> 00:37:22.330
"When I'm working on an equation

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:25.000
and I can't finish the
equation, I go play violin."

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:26.650
And people tend to forget Einstein

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:28.483
was quite a talented violinist.

00:37:29.990 --> 00:37:31.730
Some of his favorite
compositions are stuff

00:37:31.730 --> 00:37:34.350
like Bach's "Mass in B Minor"

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:36.110
or Bach's "Goldberg Variations,"

00:37:36.110 --> 00:37:38.130
which are quite mathematical.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:40.947
And so the Rabindranath Tagore said,

00:37:40.947 --> 00:37:42.440
"The problem with your Western music is,

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:43.940
you guys didn't have zero

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:45.660
and your music's too simple,

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.750
and you guys are all playing
minor chord or major chord."

00:37:48.750 --> 00:37:50.240
And if you listen to Indian music,

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.270
you can easily hear this
rich complexity of tonality

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:55.780
because they incorporated
mathematics into the music

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:58.090
at a much earlier phase in their culture.

00:37:58.090 --> 00:38:00.490
The West was still based
on the Greek model,

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:02.700
again, Pythagoras, Heraclides

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:05.430
and you can easily hear that whole tone.

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:08.950
Whenever you hear hip-hop,
techno, rock, jazz,

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:10.190
any of our current music,

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:12.570
it's still based a mainly
those Greek tuning systems.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:14.310
Minor chord, major chord.

00:38:14.310 --> 00:38:15.680
If anyone wants to get more technical,

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:18.130
it's like Lydian, Phrygian, Aeolian.

00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:19.770
Those are all ancient Greek tribes

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:21.610
that just played specific style of music.

00:38:21.610 --> 00:38:24.310
And again, I don't want
to lose half the audience

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:26.860
by getting in more technical with music.

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:30.580
But long story short, these
guys began a correspondence

00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:33.210
and their correspondence was
about physics and poetry.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:34.947
And it ended up becoming a book called,

00:38:34.947 --> 00:38:36.990
"On the Nature of Reality."

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:38.070
It's a great book where you have

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.280
one of the world's leading poets

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:41.480
talking to one of the
world's leading physicists

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:45.360
and the conversation between
that creative ebb and flow

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.683
is what inspires me for this residency.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.080
I'm a big fan of this idea of people

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:53.160
reaching across different
cultural boundaries

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:55.190
and across different silos

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:57.690
and how the 21st century is moving

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.230
is that everyone is being
put in a data bubble

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:03.220
based on your algorithmic preferences

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:05.000
and the coercion and surveillance

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:07.490
of all of these different
social media platforms.

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:10.290
So it's time to be human again maybe

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:12.360
and just be in a room with people.

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:14.140
And I guess still the pandemic,

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:16.323
and by the way, I'm fully vaccinated,

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:19.100
but I'm a big fan of thinking about

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:21.240
how do we break down these kinds of issues

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:24.610
and use them as building blocks

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:27.460
for conversation across the mediums.

00:39:27.460 --> 00:39:30.760
So given our timeframe, it's 5:42.

00:39:30.760 --> 00:39:34.100
I know that time is tight,

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:36.630
so I'm gonna just kinda
go to this one quote

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:37.710
that I really enjoyed,

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:39.237
using from Einstein, where he says,

00:39:39.237 --> 00:39:41.040
"Imagination is more
important than knowledge.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:44.527
Knowledge is limited.
Imagination encircles the world."

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.530
To me, at least, the imagination

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:49.800
is the ultimate renewable resource.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:51.790
We have to think about resilience.

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:53.600
We have to think about sustainability

00:39:53.600 --> 00:39:55.620
and we have to think
about the climate crisis

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:58.890
as basically having to
incentivize us to rethink

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:00.670
our relationship to the patterns

00:40:00.670 --> 00:40:03.120
that we've constructed
in the world around us.

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:05.290
And that's where quantum physics

00:40:05.290 --> 00:40:08.180
and, above all, thinking
about quantum computing

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:11.200
will hopefully allow us to
have a better and more robust,

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:13.810
and for what, one could argue,
a more powerful conversation

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:16.180
with how to restore some of the patterns

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:17.230
that we've disrupted.

00:40:18.688 --> 00:40:21.930
If we unpack this phrase from
the viewpoint of the arts

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:24.190
and thinking about algorithmic processes

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:27.240
and mathematics and quantum mechanics,

00:40:27.240 --> 00:40:28.840
the idea of being able
to imagine something

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:30.570
that you're unfamiliar with,

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:33.170
implies that you have to explore nuance.

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:34.920
And you have to be able to,

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:36.740
if there's a word that you haven't used

00:40:36.740 --> 00:40:37.630
and you've never heard of,

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:39.310
will you have that vocabulary?

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:40.950
If there's an equation or an approach

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:43.330
to something that you've never engaged,

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:44.547
would you be able to think about it?

00:40:44.547 --> 00:40:46.230
And one could argue philosophically

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:49.300
that you might not have
the cognitive processes

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:52.170
or structures because you've
never heard that before,

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:54.670
you've never thought of that before.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.030
The idea for the residency
is to be a provocation

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:00.690
and to apply the logic of the DJ,

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:05.280
which is about remixing,
sampling, collage, the mashup,

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:07.300
to this notion of how science and the arts

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:11.240
can have a better and
more robust conversation.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:13.290
And to me, at least, that's a beginning.

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:14.640
We're gonna see the evolution of that

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:17.100
over the next year and eventually,

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:20.133
I just want to show you, when
I got back from Antarctica,

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:22.510
I just wanna show you really quick.

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:24.960
I went from this, which is
one of the most remote places

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.810
on earth and seeing this massive,

00:41:27.810 --> 00:41:29.220
it looked like a nuclear bomb

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:30.811
had gone off in the whole continent.

00:41:30.811 --> 00:41:33.660
It's melting, I mean
let's just be clear here.

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:36.100
You can easily see the ice is collapsing.

00:41:36.100 --> 00:41:39.570
The human scale of this
is so mind boggling.

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:41.200
And so once you think about

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:44.220
the idea of scalability of experience,

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:47.310
patterns are what allow us to
navigate these kind of issues.

00:41:47.310 --> 00:41:49.453
When I got back, we had a concert,

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:53.910
where me and a group of other artists

00:41:55.840 --> 00:41:59.440
did a series of music pieces

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:02.510
and each of us did a different style.

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:04.600
I've worked with the
hip hop group The Roots,

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:06.740
which I'm not sure if you
guys are familiar with,

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:09.840
and there also another group
called The Flaming Lips.

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:11.840
And each of us did a different approach.

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:14.410
So I did one style, they did one style

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:17.200
and I just want to show you the concert.

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:20.470
It's a lot of photos here,
I always forget which...

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:24.440
But essentially the concert
was one of the largest,

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:26.000
it was for Earth Day

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:28.930
and what we did was
try as much as possible

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:33.010
to engage the notion of
how people would think

00:42:33.010 --> 00:42:35.420
about Earth Day and disruption

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:38.720
and giving people better
tools for critical thinking.

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:39.700
To me, at least, that's one of

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:41.840
the most powerful things we can have.

00:42:41.840 --> 00:42:46.070
and it really gives us a
sensibility that the imagination,

00:42:46.070 --> 00:42:46.903
like I was saying earlier,

00:42:46.903 --> 00:42:48.950
is the ultimate renewable resource.

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:51.360
How do we get people to rethink

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:54.133
the process of re-imagining
what is possible?

00:42:55.750 --> 00:42:56.690
For one reason or other,

00:42:56.690 --> 00:42:59.600
I can't seem to find the concert photo,

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:02.120
but it was a big concert.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:03.480
I don't know if you guys
know The Flaming Lips

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:07.483
or The Roots, it was a popular show.

00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:14.770
One other thing, let's see,

00:43:14.770 --> 00:43:16.470
trying to make sure to
give you guys some context.

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:20.520
This is what I do normally
like kids are rockin' out.

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:23.480
That's about 10,000 people
in front of the stage.

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:24.470
Amusing if all these girls

00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:26.220
were throwing their bra on the stage,

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:29.700
I'm very un-PC to say that, but it's real.

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:32.113
So this is the view from the DJ booth.

00:43:32.113 --> 00:43:34.190
I'm normally, playing beats and people

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:36.440
are rockin' and stuff like that.

00:43:36.440 --> 00:43:39.640
So this is not what I'm
gonna be doing with Yale.

00:43:39.640 --> 00:43:41.837
Although maybe we can
make some quantum hip-hop

00:43:41.837 --> 00:43:42.783
and we'll figure,

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:47.290
but the fun part about this
is that it is a social process

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:49.830
and to be able to have fun

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:52.940
and also be able to get
the conversation moving

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:54.300
outside of the silos

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:57.150
and being able to break those silos down.

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:58.260
That's what's gonna be

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:00.763
the next several months
of what's going on.

00:44:03.570 --> 00:44:05.730
The concert that we did for Earth Day

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:07.400
was something like that,

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:10.550
but all the music that I had made

00:44:10.550 --> 00:44:13.970
was generated by the
recordings I made in Antarctica

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:18.230
and amusingly and strangely enough,

00:44:18.230 --> 00:44:21.040
when you start to think about project,

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:23.080
the research that goes into the project

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:26.570
is what makes part of
it so intriguing to me.

00:44:26.570 --> 00:44:31.390
So I'll be coming up here
over the next several months

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:34.500
and having a series of engagements

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:40.940
with scientists, with creatives
and, above all, with people.

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:42.147
I'm just trying to figure, where is that?

00:44:42.147 --> 00:44:44.043
I have so many freaking photos.

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:48.050
There's various videos
I could show you guys,

00:44:48.050 --> 00:44:49.960
there's various, oh here we go.

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:52.640
So for this one, we had
about a million plus people

00:44:52.640 --> 00:44:54.550
and that's me DJing.

00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:56.280
We filled the entire,

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:57.650
this is at the DC mall,

00:44:57.650 --> 00:44:59.240
it's in a massive space.

00:44:59.240 --> 00:45:02.090
So all the music I was playing
was generated in Antarctica

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:03.850
and I took the sound of ice.

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:06.230
I took the sound of the
temperature differentials

00:45:06.230 --> 00:45:07.980
and then made electronic music out of it.

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:09.360
And then The Flaming Lips came out

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.880
and they did their part
of the concert where,

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:14.010
they're like an art rock band.

00:45:14.010 --> 00:45:14.920
I'm more hip-hop.

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:17.810
Again, the idea was to
build bridges between styles

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:18.810
and then The Roots came out

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:21.090
and they're more straight
up normal hip-hop.

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:24.380
But I usually do
large-scale music festivals.

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:26.910
And the audience was into it

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:28.670
because the whole concert was based on

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:32.180
the sounds of nature that had been cut up,

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:36.170
sliced and diced into electronic music.

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:38.270
So you could party to the sound

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:40.263
of climate change and Anthropocene era.

00:45:41.146 --> 00:45:43.970
(all chuckling)

00:45:43.970 --> 00:45:46.280
The good news out of that is
that the audience was able

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:48.420
to relate to the fact that
you could actually make

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:50.940
an acoustic portrait of climate change

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:52.720
and that helped raise awareness.

00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:54.850
And we also had a political engagement

00:45:54.850 --> 00:45:56.360
with raising more awareness about

00:45:56.360 --> 00:45:59.000
climate change and climate justice.

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:01.820
To me, that's a really
critical issue of our time

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:05.860
and it's gonna be, I would be surprised,

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:07.960
if our species makes it out
of this century, personally.

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:11.583
I know that sounds a little
cynical, but it's pretty bleak.

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:16.340
So there's plenty of other
stuff I could chat about.

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:19.370
I do want to leave time. It's 5:50.

00:46:19.370 --> 00:46:21.930
- [Audience Member] Well, could
you give us a little sound

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:24.430
since you're keep showing us pictures?

00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:28.220
You did sounds, are you
gonna give us a little sound?

00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:31.530
- Sure, I mean, I was gonna play a mashup

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:33.710
just to give you a sense
of how I was scratching.

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:36.060
And also there's plenty
of video of me doing stuff

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:37.710
on the internet anyway.

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.290
By the way, I do want to,

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:40.240
and thank you for bringing it up

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:42.450
and I will play one thing in one moment,

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:44.140
but I do want to emphasize, I mean,

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:46.270
this is like, just going to...

00:46:46.270 --> 00:46:48.230
It's not everyday you
have a DJ from New York

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:49.780
go to the middle of Antarctica.

00:46:50.854 --> 00:46:51.687
- [Audience Member] I thought
you were going to say to you

00:46:51.687 --> 00:46:54.360
that you hate it.
(audience laughing)

00:46:54.360 --> 00:46:56.230
- You guys have quite a
few DJs coming through,

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:57.063
I'm pretty sure.

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:02.750
But the idea here is, think
about this as a pattern

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:03.760
that's in disruption.

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:06.400
You're seeing the collapse
of an entire glacial shelf.

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:08.250
As a matter of fact, a lot
of scientists are saying

00:47:08.250 --> 00:47:11.690
that essentially this entire area

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:14.680
is now collapsing into the ocean.

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:17.080
When I was there, it took
about eight hours to walk

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:20.240
from that water all the way up
to the side of this mountain.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:22.460
And I was very hot by
it, because you're moving

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.760
and by the time I got to
the top of the mountain,

00:47:24.760 --> 00:47:26.800
I'd been wearing all this gear

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:28.790
and I was, "Oh my God, it's frying."

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:32.440
So each of these projects requires

00:47:32.440 --> 00:47:36.683
a tremendous amount of editing,
recording, documentation,

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:40.570
but when I got back from going to this

00:47:40.570 --> 00:47:42.980
and then had the concert for Earth Day,

00:47:42.980 --> 00:47:44.130
the audience doesn't see all that.

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:46.300
They want a party or something

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:50.780
that's kind of immersive and fun.

00:47:50.780 --> 00:47:52.820
For me, at least, this
idea of the research

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:55.393
is where I derive personal satisfaction.

00:47:56.540 --> 00:47:58.140
Let me play you guys.

00:47:58.140 --> 00:48:00.370
I mean, I have lots of albums out too,

00:48:00.370 --> 00:48:02.810
but at the Antarctica project
is particularly resonant

00:48:02.810 --> 00:48:03.810
with Yale right now,

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:05.580
because I think that
a lot of it's gonna be

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:07.980
about its conversations with scientists.

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:11.030
And by the way, Antarctica
is the only place on earth

00:48:11.030 --> 00:48:12.030
with no government,

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:14.380
so I made the entire project open source.

00:48:14.380 --> 00:48:17.220
And the idea is that you
can take the climate data

00:48:17.220 --> 00:48:19.090
and make your own remixes.

00:48:19.090 --> 00:48:21.130
All of this is available on my website,

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:22.870
if you go to www.DJspooky.com

00:48:22.870 --> 00:48:24.770
and I'll just show you really quickly.

00:48:26.930 --> 00:48:30.330
The idea was as much as
possible to reference...

00:48:31.940 --> 00:48:32.773
Let's see.

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:35.593
Yeah. Okay.

00:48:35.593 --> 00:48:37.760
So what I did was take those
temperature differentials.

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:39.580
This is temperature over several years.

00:48:39.580 --> 00:48:41.437
This is you just seeing
a small sliver of it

00:48:41.437 --> 00:48:43.160
and then take the
temperature differentials,

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:46.060
assign them to music tone patterns.

00:48:46.060 --> 00:48:48.800
Then use those patterns to
generate, what you call,

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:50.480
an algorithmic musical transcription

00:48:50.480 --> 00:48:53.520
of weather over a long period of time.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:55.940
And then use that to generate music

00:48:55.940 --> 00:48:58.490
based on these different
algorithmic transcription.

00:48:59.336 --> 00:49:02.380
Again, I can get more
technical if everybody wants,

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:05.350
but basically it's patterns

00:49:05.350 --> 00:49:07.730
that you can put into different tempos.

00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:11.450
So house music, techno, dubstep, hip-hop,

00:49:11.450 --> 00:49:12.530
they're all patterns.

00:49:12.530 --> 00:49:15.980
So once you have the basic
pattern of the weather

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:17.690
and the temperature differentials,

00:49:17.690 --> 00:49:21.700
I was able to take that and
make an open source album.

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:23.180
The album is on my website.

00:49:23.180 --> 00:49:26.170
If you just go to
www.DJspooky.com/antarctica,

00:49:26.170 --> 00:49:27.610
all of the data is there

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:30.460
and it was done with Darkness
Cold Regions Research Labs.

00:49:32.604 --> 00:49:33.500
Each of these products,

00:49:33.500 --> 00:49:35.800
I could do an entire talk
on just for that product,

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:37.660
but I do want to show you some of that.

00:49:37.660 --> 00:49:39.690
We're lifting the hood
of the car a little bit.

00:49:39.690 --> 00:49:41.943
So this is just a little backdrop.

00:49:43.750 --> 00:49:46.060
Each of these products could be full on,

00:49:46.060 --> 00:49:48.540
but I do want to just
show you really quickly.

00:49:48.540 --> 00:49:50.000
The inspiration for that project

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:52.180
was this gentleman, Johannes Kepler.

00:49:52.180 --> 00:49:54.870
And basically he was on his way home

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:56.560
in the middle of a snow storm

00:49:56.560 --> 00:50:01.130
and he basically realized
that a piece of ice

00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:02.240
had landed on his sleeve

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:04.210
and he wrote the first equations

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:06.113
defining the geometry of ice.

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:10.403
It's pretty wild.

00:50:10.403 --> 00:50:12.410
This is in the year 1611

00:50:12.410 --> 00:50:14.710
and he's on his way home in
the middle of a snow storm

00:50:14.710 --> 00:50:17.580
and this, like a piece of
ice lands on his sleeve

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:20.150
and he realizes how geometric it is.

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:24.280
And basically ends up thinking
about the geometry of ice

00:50:24.280 --> 00:50:26.817
as the foundations for this book,

00:50:26.817 --> 00:50:28.790
"The Six Sides of a Snowflake."

00:50:28.790 --> 00:50:31.250
It's the first time you have
a mathematical interpretation

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:33.010
of the geometry of ice

00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:35.200
and you realize you can
see the geometry of ice

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:36.953
pretty clearly, pretty easily,

00:50:37.903 --> 00:50:40.500
and what you're seeing is
permutations of the changing same.

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:41.630
This is literally the same,

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:43.370
if you took the same equation,

00:50:43.370 --> 00:50:45.830
you will be able to
generate different things.

00:50:45.830 --> 00:50:48.400
So I was like, "Oh,
let's take the equation."

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:51.480
So what I did was run it
through different patterns

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:54.150
and I took a capillary material,

00:50:54.150 --> 00:50:58.500
used it to generate more and
more fractal relationships

00:50:58.500 --> 00:51:00.940
and you realize after enough computation,

00:51:00.940 --> 00:51:02.690
you can have synthetic ice.

00:51:02.690 --> 00:51:05.970
And so basically each of these projects,

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:07.290
there's the science component

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:08.750
and then there's the fun component.

00:51:08.750 --> 00:51:10.820
So I need to, as an artist,

00:51:10.820 --> 00:51:13.472
I was trying to sort of balance the two,

00:51:13.472 --> 00:51:16.860
because if you make it too much,

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:18.437
then you lose some of the audience.

00:51:18.437 --> 00:51:19.680
And if you make it too little,

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:21.460
then you lose some of the audience.

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:24.313
The good idea is to
just be able to balance.

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:27.420
For example, like I was
showing you earlier,

00:51:28.640 --> 00:51:29.740
eventually, for example,

00:51:29.740 --> 00:51:31.160
this is one of the installation pieces

00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:33.450
we had at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

00:51:33.450 --> 00:51:34.630
And I took the geometry of the ice.

00:51:34.630 --> 00:51:37.060
I worked with a group of architects

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:39.210
and we developed a series of sculptures

00:51:39.210 --> 00:51:40.610
that we projected on.

00:51:40.610 --> 00:51:42.170
That was like the art version.

00:51:42.170 --> 00:51:43.980
And then that other one

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:46.220
where like a million plus
people for Earth Day,

00:51:46.220 --> 00:51:48.753
that was the party version.

00:51:49.780 --> 00:51:52.260
There's plenty of other
stuff I could show you guys,

00:51:52.260 --> 00:51:55.840
but I just want to wrap up
because time, it's 5:55.

00:51:55.840 --> 00:51:57.840
This is a very, I think,

00:51:57.840 --> 00:52:00.490
inspirational piece from Richard Dawkins,

00:52:00.490 --> 00:52:03.180
where he says science is
the poetry of reality.

00:52:03.180 --> 00:52:04.730
I began my discussion by showing you guys

00:52:04.730 --> 00:52:08.230
the conversation between
Einstein and Rabindranath Tagore.

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:09.780
And I'll end with Dawkins,

00:52:09.780 --> 00:52:11.430
where this simple phrase is,

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:15.260
I think, an elegant distillation
of some of the ideas

00:52:15.260 --> 00:52:17.230
that are gonna be inspiring the residency

00:52:17.230 --> 00:52:18.730
over the next several months.

00:52:18.730 --> 00:52:21.050
And so the idea is to work with you all,

00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:22.940
work with the Institute

00:52:22.940 --> 00:52:25.930
and see what we can do
from the conversation.

00:52:25.930 --> 00:52:28.530
And again, to go back
to the ancient Greeks,

00:52:28.530 --> 00:52:30.610
they would call this
a dialectical process.

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:34.280
You have thesis, antithesis and synthesis.

00:52:34.280 --> 00:52:36.890
So the gentleman asked for some music.

00:52:36.890 --> 00:52:38.810
I can play you,

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:41.460
there's different versions of everything

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:43.220
because that's what the
idea of DJ culture is,

00:52:43.220 --> 00:52:44.960
it's remix and mashup.

00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:48.160
So I just want to play you
one that I find amusing,

00:52:48.160 --> 00:52:51.160
but it's not definitive of my style.

00:52:51.160 --> 00:52:53.750
A lot of people, like
if you hear The Roots,

00:52:53.750 --> 00:52:54.670
you hear their style.

00:52:54.670 --> 00:52:57.660
Or if you hear Radiohead,
you hear their style.

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.730
For DJ culture, anything goes.

00:52:59.730 --> 00:53:02.300
I can take any style and flip it around.

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:03.310
So I'll play you,

00:53:03.310 --> 00:53:05.163
because I grew up in the ancient 1990s

00:53:05.163 --> 00:53:06.820
when you guys weren't born,

00:53:06.820 --> 00:53:08.640
this is a kind of a fun one.

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:10.413
I'll just play you a quick clip.

00:53:10.413 --> 00:53:13.996
(uptempo electronic music)

00:53:40.400 --> 00:53:41.790
So that's not what,

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:43.020
you guys are getting the art mix.

00:53:43.020 --> 00:53:44.334
That's the party mix.

00:53:44.334 --> 00:53:47.010
(audience laughing)

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:48.960
Just to clarify there.

00:53:48.960 --> 00:53:50.710
That would work for

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:54.153
concerts and parties like this.

00:53:55.010 --> 00:53:57.660
You guys are getting a different mix.

00:53:57.660 --> 00:53:59.646
You guys are getting to academics mix.

00:53:59.646 --> 00:54:04.646
(audience laughing)
(audience chattering)

00:54:06.910 --> 00:54:09.860
So that one, you need a big
sound system and a lot of bass.

00:54:10.790 --> 00:54:13.320
- [Florian] We're trying to
work with the shopping center

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:16.523
to try to organize it for the fans.

00:54:16.523 --> 00:54:17.820
- This is the fun mix.

00:54:17.820 --> 00:54:21.850
So I'm giving you guys the
science and stuff like that.

00:54:21.850 --> 00:54:23.300
So just so you're aware,

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:26.550
like I said, I inhabit
different ecosystems.

00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:31.550
So this probably you got
to have lasers and fog,

00:54:31.880 --> 00:54:33.020
maybe you guys would come in the room,

00:54:33.020 --> 00:54:34.923
I've got lasers going or something.

00:54:34.923 --> 00:54:38.300
- We have some lasers.
(audience laughing)

00:54:38.300 --> 00:54:39.603
- Physics is lasers.

00:54:40.950 --> 00:54:44.070
All right, so thank you
guys so much for your time

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:45.650
and just it's...

00:54:45.650 --> 00:54:47.936
Florian, how do you want it, should we?

00:54:47.936 --> 00:54:48.769
- [Florian] If you have any question,

00:54:48.769 --> 00:54:49.730
please feel free to do it to ask them.

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:51.820
I'll monitor the chats to make sure

00:54:51.820 --> 00:54:55.600
that we have the question
from the audience here.

00:54:55.600 --> 00:54:56.433
- Okay.

00:54:57.569 --> 00:55:00.736
(audience applauding)

00:55:04.935 --> 00:55:08.587
- [Florian] Do you have a question?

00:55:08.587 --> 00:55:09.420
- [Audience Member] I was wondering,

00:55:09.420 --> 00:55:11.190
when you collect, let's
say weather patterns

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:13.610
and it's got this interesting pattern

00:55:13.610 --> 00:55:15.090
and then you transform
into the acoustic domain,

00:55:15.090 --> 00:55:17.700
do you have any idea how it's gonna sound

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:19.980
or is that always a surprise?

00:55:19.980 --> 00:55:22.323
- You know, there's a couple approaches.

00:55:23.330 --> 00:55:25.380
I've worked with the mathematician,

00:55:25.380 --> 00:55:27.083
I'll just show you really quickly,

00:55:28.960 --> 00:55:31.750
Stephen Wolfram who's probably,

00:55:31.750 --> 00:55:35.100
a pretty legendary mathematician.

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:36.093
And just show you.

00:55:39.978 --> 00:55:41.670
This is a project where you

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:44.730
can basically compute any style.

00:55:44.730 --> 00:55:47.160
And hip-hop, techno, dubstep,

00:55:47.160 --> 00:55:49.490
they're usually at four-four
tempo, for example.

00:55:49.490 --> 00:55:51.650
Which is relatively trivial to compute.

00:55:51.650 --> 00:55:53.210
Then you have minor chord,

00:55:53.210 --> 00:55:55.680
usually mostly hip-hop and
techno are minor chords,

00:55:55.680 --> 00:55:56.513
et cetera.

00:55:56.513 --> 00:55:58.420
So you were able to
actually click on these

00:55:58.420 --> 00:56:00.050
and start using it to generate midi

00:56:00.050 --> 00:56:01.160
and export the midi

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:02.720
and then pull that into an editing,

00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:05.065
Digital Audio Workstation, DAW.

00:56:05.065 --> 00:56:06.630
So when I'm working on a project,

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.963
I normally don't have
the end result in mind.

00:56:10.050 --> 00:56:12.363
The process of making the material,

00:56:14.416 --> 00:56:16.180
it depends on the situation.

00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:18.350
Like if I'm going for,

00:56:18.350 --> 00:56:19.693
you guys are more of like,

00:56:21.640 --> 00:56:23.120
I don't view today as a party mix.

00:56:23.120 --> 00:56:27.710
So I would probably not
be, but I have other,

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:28.920
that's what I was mentioning earlier,

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:30.143
I have many styles.

00:56:31.020 --> 00:56:32.200
I'm using this as an example,

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:34.570
because he was able to
compute almost every aspect

00:56:34.570 --> 00:56:37.180
of using an algorithmic approach

00:56:37.180 --> 00:56:39.800
to all of the major formats of music.

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:42.910
But for me at least, I'm
very interested in thinking

00:56:42.910 --> 00:56:45.460
about existing in multiple ecosystems.

00:56:45.460 --> 00:56:48.650
So hip-hop might not
work for certain things

00:56:48.650 --> 00:56:50.480
and then house music might
work for other things

00:56:50.480 --> 00:56:52.880
and classical music might
work for other things.

00:56:53.850 --> 00:56:58.450
And, above all, the notion
of fidelity to the data

00:56:58.450 --> 00:57:00.780
is not my foundation.

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:02.940
A lot of people would say you should only,

00:57:02.940 --> 00:57:06.563
there are certain other
composers like Ryuichi Akada,

00:57:07.460 --> 00:57:09.450
I have a feeling he's not well-known here.

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:11.700
He's a Japanese composer,
he's interesting.

00:57:11.700 --> 00:57:14.200
He'll do visual installations

00:57:14.200 --> 00:57:16.460
based on a granular synthesis.

00:57:16.460 --> 00:57:17.787
And he's very specific about that.

00:57:17.787 --> 00:57:19.440
And he projects it large scale,

00:57:19.440 --> 00:57:21.160
like small dots and stuff.

00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:23.433
He's very Japanese, German minimalism.

00:57:24.810 --> 00:57:28.740
That's again, not like a
party or he has very...

00:57:28.740 --> 00:57:29.657
I'm using him as an example,

00:57:29.657 --> 00:57:32.793
but there's other artists doing sound art.

00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:36.400
Usually they avoid beats.

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:37.460
You'll go to a gallery

00:57:37.460 --> 00:57:39.260
and there'll be some noise

00:57:39.260 --> 00:57:42.720
or some sort of glitchy or
skipping CD sound or something,

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:43.960
but they never have beats.

00:57:43.960 --> 00:57:46.783
And so I, on the other hand, go more for,

00:57:48.540 --> 00:57:51.820
incorporating beats with the science.

00:57:51.820 --> 00:57:54.350
But again, I'm never sure quite

00:57:54.350 --> 00:57:56.490
if it's gonna be an exact match,

00:57:56.490 --> 00:57:58.633
but it should be a good beginning point.

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:02.440
- [Audience Member]
Yes, we were wondering,

00:58:02.440 --> 00:58:06.030
so you were saying that
with quantum computing

00:58:06.030 --> 00:58:07.930
and things you were doing here at Yale

00:58:08.813 --> 00:58:10.360
it comes with a lot of opportunity

00:58:10.360 --> 00:58:11.193
and I was wondering,

00:58:11.193 --> 00:58:14.500
because also coming with that,

00:58:14.500 --> 00:58:16.730
is it a little responsibility?

00:58:16.730 --> 00:58:21.410
What we choose to make
(indistinct) future,

00:58:21.410 --> 00:58:26.410
but it seems that this is
a very nice opportunity

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:30.733
to make art and philosophy.

00:58:34.090 --> 00:58:35.580
Are you thinking in your project

00:58:35.580 --> 00:58:37.640
that you're going be able to incorporate

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:39.171
that sense of responsibility

00:58:39.171 --> 00:58:43.553
and kind of ethical questions
about what we work on at all?

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:47.970
- Yeah, I'm fascinated with
entanglement, for example.

00:58:47.970 --> 00:58:50.950
And I'm going to use the
idea of quantum entanglement

00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:52.703
as a foundation for,

00:58:52.703 --> 00:58:55.370
it's part of the residency to look up

00:58:55.370 --> 00:58:57.730
and Einstein called it
spooky action at a distance,

00:58:57.730 --> 00:58:58.800
for example.

00:58:58.800 --> 00:59:00.930
So when you're thinking about the ethics

00:59:00.930 --> 00:59:03.610
of being a composer, talking to a sciences

00:59:03.610 --> 00:59:05.600
or a composer engaging with

00:59:07.610 --> 00:59:10.370
things outside of their normal domain,

00:59:10.370 --> 00:59:11.950
you have to imagine that

00:59:11.950 --> 00:59:14.150
it's the beginning of a conversation

00:59:14.150 --> 00:59:17.623
and how that conversation
goes is the dialectic.

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:21.380
This is just beginning

00:59:21.380 --> 00:59:23.260
so the next, I think,

00:59:23.260 --> 00:59:24.773
is it 12 or 15 months?

00:59:26.102 --> 00:59:27.650
- 12.
- 12 months.

00:59:27.650 --> 00:59:28.810
For me, that's millennia.

00:59:28.810 --> 00:59:31.483
Normally I do a project in
like a month, it may be,

00:59:32.350 --> 00:59:34.360
so I will be coming up to Yale

00:59:34.360 --> 00:59:36.340
and also we're going to
host a series of events

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:37.433
in New York as well,

00:59:38.290 --> 00:59:40.440
which you guys are all welcome to join.

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:42.017
We will have, you know,

00:59:42.017 --> 00:59:46.110
I'll have more firm date for
some of the New York events,

00:59:46.110 --> 00:59:48.163
but the idea is the dialectic.

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:52.430
And I'm very intrigued with
how the dialectical process

00:59:52.430 --> 00:59:56.260
is at the heart, both of
science and replicate ability.

00:59:56.260 --> 00:59:59.240
If you go back to again, the
ancient Greeks doing, you know,

00:59:59.240 --> 01:00:02.030
experiments and trying
to figure out things,

01:00:02.030 --> 01:00:04.650
I'm inspired by that because
it was like they had,

01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:06.250
they were just trying to figure it out.

01:00:06.250 --> 01:00:09.190
And it was kind of like, you
know, if you're Archimedes,

01:00:09.190 --> 01:00:11.020
you're like I can move the entire world

01:00:11.020 --> 01:00:12.730
if I put a lever in the right place.

01:00:12.730 --> 01:00:14.230
And okay, how do you calculate that?

01:00:14.230 --> 01:00:17.380
And he calculates the entire
circumference of the world.

01:00:17.380 --> 01:00:20.650
You know, there's all sorts
of intriguing possibilities,

01:00:20.650 --> 01:00:24.690
but the ethics of science
versus the arts is very,

01:00:24.690 --> 01:00:27.870
it's a very ambiguous place right now.

01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:31.360
One could argue that the
imagination can be weaponized too,

01:00:31.360 --> 01:00:33.540
which some people like, for example,

01:00:33.540 --> 01:00:35.390
if you're talking about entanglement,

01:00:36.670 --> 01:00:39.040
they've already got
some satellites in orbit

01:00:39.040 --> 01:00:40.950
using quantum entanglement.

01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:43.340
The Chinese launched
satellite a little while ago,

01:00:43.340 --> 01:00:46.220
America has launched
a military satellites,

01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:48.580
but they're supposed to
be unbreakable encryption

01:00:48.580 --> 01:00:51.523
because the entanglement of
all sorts of other processes

01:00:51.523 --> 01:00:52.693
that go into that.

01:00:56.220 --> 01:00:58.700
Military applications of quantum physics

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:00.513
is definitely a serious issue.

01:01:01.430 --> 01:01:04.410
But one also has to
remember if the Americans

01:01:04.410 --> 01:01:05.640
aren't doing it, the Israelis are doing,

01:01:05.640 --> 01:01:07.390
if not the Israelis then the Chinese,

01:01:07.390 --> 01:01:09.220
and it's not the Chinese,
it's the Russians.

01:01:09.220 --> 01:01:11.620
And everybody is trying
to figure this stuff out.

01:01:11.620 --> 01:01:14.730
And usually it's for some military stuff.

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:18.040
So, as an artist waltzing
through the room, you know,

01:01:18.040 --> 01:01:22.020
I don't have machine gun or
extra lasers to fire anybody,

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:27.020
but it's the beginning
of a conversation to me

01:01:27.310 --> 01:01:30.740
about how the arts can help make a better

01:01:30.740 --> 01:01:33.180
and non-military approach to things.

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:35.480
A lot of this stuff is being
weaponized immediately,

01:01:35.480 --> 01:01:39.913
like military funding
for quantum researchers.

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:42.560
It's pretty serious.

01:01:42.560 --> 01:01:44.250
I'm not familiar with all the numbers,

01:01:44.250 --> 01:01:46.440
but I know it's a very
significant amount of money

01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:48.150
being put out a government,

01:01:48.150 --> 01:01:51.350
all major governance or their military,

01:01:51.350 --> 01:01:53.690
for like artificial
intelligence, quantum computing.

01:01:53.690 --> 01:01:55.933
And so, yeah, so.

01:01:57.130 --> 01:01:59.760
- [Florian] We have a
question in the chat here,

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:02.820
but they said that something
I experienced is very cool

01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:05.160
and I appreciate your
focus on climate change.

01:02:05.160 --> 01:02:09.370
They advised to work with
the (indistinct) this year.

01:02:09.370 --> 01:02:11.100
And then also they have a question,

01:02:11.100 --> 01:02:13.110
how do you connect to a
human or human sounds?

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:16.383
And we thought to integrate
these into your music.

01:02:16.383 --> 01:02:20.460
- I'll answer part two first
and then go to this first part.

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:23.090
I wasn't interested in
human sounds in Antarctica.

01:02:23.090 --> 01:02:28.090
I was interested in the sounds
more of nature's processes

01:02:28.100 --> 01:02:29.850
because everything has rhythm.

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:32.990
Even the absence of a
rhythm is kind of a rhythm.

01:02:32.990 --> 01:02:37.960
So the wind, water,
even neutrinos, we took,

01:02:37.960 --> 01:02:39.840
I tried to experiment with it,

01:02:39.840 --> 01:02:42.630
that was part of an ice cube in Antarctica

01:02:42.630 --> 01:02:46.920
that's a neutrino measurement
center where they're looking

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:49.540
at the impact of
neutrinos through the ice.

01:02:49.540 --> 01:02:50.653
That was interesting.

01:02:52.070 --> 01:02:52.903
Human sounds.

01:02:52.903 --> 01:02:54.930
There's a lot of other artists doing that.

01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:59.290
So, you know, if you just
Google like sound art,

01:02:59.290 --> 01:03:01.240
there's zillions of people doing stuff.

01:03:03.033 --> 01:03:04.750
In fact, there's one
actually, who I was working.

01:03:04.750 --> 01:03:07.290
I do like Markus Beuhler at MIT.

01:03:07.290 --> 01:03:09.500
He's been doing stuff
with the sound of DNA

01:03:09.500 --> 01:03:12.300
and he's been doing,
his most infamous piece

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:14.820
was he took the DNA of the COVID virus

01:03:14.820 --> 01:03:16.520
and made a series of
electronic music pieces

01:03:16.520 --> 01:03:19.860
out of the COVID, you know,

01:03:19.860 --> 01:03:23.680
which it sounds like
experimental electronic music,

01:03:23.680 --> 01:03:24.980
like no beats or anything.

01:03:26.480 --> 01:03:28.070
So the other thing is, yeah,

01:03:28.070 --> 01:03:30.880
I would love to work with
y'all's environmental department.

01:03:30.880 --> 01:03:33.540
In fact, hopefully we'll reach out to them

01:03:33.540 --> 01:03:35.090
over the next couple of months.

01:03:37.000 --> 01:03:39.960
- [Florian] Any further
questions from the audience?

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:44.723
If not, let's thank-
(audience applauding)

01:03:48.960 --> 01:03:50.150
- I do want to just mention, everybody,

01:03:50.150 --> 01:03:51.250
everything I'm talking about,

01:03:51.250 --> 01:03:54.060
I'm a big fan of what you
call open source culture.

01:03:54.060 --> 01:03:57.170
So much of the work that
I'm doing is open source.

01:03:57.170 --> 01:03:59.710
And on my website, there's articles,

01:03:59.710 --> 01:04:01.950
there's download material.

01:04:01.950 --> 01:04:05.323
There's articles that books,
my books are with MIT.

01:04:06.200 --> 01:04:09.200
Lately I've been really interested
in this idea of curating.

01:04:10.481 --> 01:04:13.311
I just curated a big
exhibition about Afrofuturism,

01:04:13.311 --> 01:04:16.530
which is another art
movement that's pretty cool.

01:04:16.530 --> 01:04:20.130
And yeah, I'm a big fan
of the dialect process.

01:04:20.130 --> 01:04:22.020
So thank you guys so much for coming out

01:04:22.020 --> 01:04:24.973
and I hope to see you guys
over the next couple months.

